@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex introduction
@cindex memory store, shared
@cindex pipelines, multi-model
@cindex verbatim memory

Engram is a shared verbatim chat memory store for multi-model LLM
pipelines.  It keeps decisions, facts, and design rationale in a single
SQLite file --- with FTS5 full-text search --- that multiple models and
agents can read from and write to.  Memory persists across the stages
of a pipeline without requiring LLM calls to encode or retrieve
context: what was stored is returned verbatim.

@cindex SQLite
@cindex FTS5
@cindex full-text search

@section The three surfaces

@cindex surfaces, the three
Engram exposes one store through three surfaces.  All three dispatch to
the same underlying storage, so a memory written on any surface is
immediately visible to the other two.

@table @asis
@item CLI
@cindex CLI surface
The @command{engram} command-line tool, for shell scripts, hooks, and
direct use.  @xref{Invocation}.

@item MCP
@cindex MCP surface
A Model Context Protocol server over stdio (@command{engram mcp}), for
Claude Code, Codex, and other MCP clients.  @xref{MCP Server}.

@item HTTP
@cindex HTTP surface
A local-only HTTP API (@command{engram serve}), for any HTTP client:
@command{curl}, Kimi, Ollama Cloud, and so on.  @xref{HTTP API}.
@end table

@section Memories and rules

@cindex memories versus rules
Engram stores two kinds of durable state.  A @dfn{memory} records what
happened --- a decision, a fact, a piece of rationale --- scoped to a
project, task, or run identifier.  A @dfn{rule} states what must keep
being true: durable project policy that outlives any one session.
Rules are covered in their own chapter (@pxref{Rules}).

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@cindex invocation
@cindex command line

The general form is:

@example
engram [@var{global-options}] @var{command} [@var{command-options}] [@var{args}]
@end example

@section Global options

@cindex global options
@cindex options, global

@table @code
@item --db @var{path}
@cindex database path
Path to the shared SQLite database.  Defaults to @file{engram.db}; the
@env{ENGRAM_DB} environment variable overrides the default.  All agents
should point at the same file (or the same @command{engram serve}
instance) to actually share memory.

@item --json
@cindex JSON output
Alias for @code{--format json}.

@item --format @var{format}
@cindex output formats
Machine output format: @code{json}, @code{jsonl}, or @code{csv}.
Overrides mode auto-detection.  (@code{yaml} is deferred.)

@item --no-color
@cindex colors, disabling
Disable colored output.  The @env{NO_COLOR} environment variable has
the same effect.

@item --accessible
@cindex accessible output
@cindex accessibility
Accessible output (Standard @U{00A7}18): plain linear text, no color,
status tags.  Also enabled by @env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}=1;
@env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}=0 disables auto-detection, but this flag still
wins.

@item --no-track
@cindex access tracking
@cindex no-track flag
Read-only auditing: do not update access counts on reads.  Without
this flag, @command{recall}, @command{search}, @command{context}, and
@command{get} bump each @emph{returned} memory's internal
@code{access_count} and @code{last_accessed_at} columns --- the decay
signal for @command{engram consolidate --report}.  The columns never
appear in any output, and the MCP/HTTP surfaces have no opt-out of
their own: agent reads are exactly what the tracking measures.
@end table

@section Output modes

@cindex output modes
@cindex machine mode
@cindex mode cascade

Engram is a dual-mode CLI: human-readable text on a terminal, machine
output everywhere else.  The mode cascade, from strongest to weakest:

@enumerate
@item
An explicit @code{--format} or @code{--json} flag always wins.

@item
Otherwise, machine (JSON) mode is selected when @env{AI_AGENT} or
@env{AGENT} is set non-empty, or when @env{CI} is truthy.

@item
Otherwise, machine (JSON) mode is selected when stdout is not a TTY.
@end enumerate

Human mode therefore requires a real TTY on stdout.

@subsection The metadata envelope

@cindex envelope, metadata
@cindex metadata envelope
Every machine-mode response is wrapped in an envelope:

@verbatim
{"metadata":{"tool":"engram","version":"0.2.0","command":"recall",
             "timestamp":"2026-08-01T00:00:00Z"},
 "data":...}
@end verbatim

The @code{metadata} object carries @code{tool}, @code{version},
@code{command}, and an ISO 8601 UTC @code{timestamp}; it optionally
carries @code{tool_agent} (when an agent environment was detected),
@code{dry_run} (on dry runs), and @code{budget} (when token budgeting
was requested --- see @command{engram recall} below).  The payload is
under @code{data}.

@subsection JSON Lines output

@cindex JSONL
@cindex JSON Lines
With @code{--format jsonl}, the first line is the metadata envelope
with @code{"data": null}; then, for array results, one line follows per
record --- or, for a single object, one line with that object.

@subsection CSV output

@cindex CSV
@cindex RFC 4180
With @code{--format csv}, the data records are emitted as RFC 4180 CSV
rows on stdout, with the header row derived from the first record's
keys.  The metadata envelope goes to stderr as one JSON line.

@subsection Errors and exit codes

@cindex errors, structured
@cindex exit codes
In machine mode, errors are a single line of JSON on stderr:

@verbatim
{"error":{"code":"...","exit_code":2,"message":"...","hint":"...",
          "timestamp":"...","command":"...","docs_url":"..."}}
@end verbatim

Exit codes:

@table @code
@item 0
Success.
@item 1
Internal or storage error.
@item 2
Invalid argument.
@item 3
Not found.
@item 5
Conflict --- the supersede target was already superseded.
@end table

@section engram remember

@cindex remember command
@cindex storing a memory

@example
engram remember --agent @var{name} --scope @var{id} [--role @var{role}] [--supersedes @var{id}] [--dry-run] [@var{content}]
@end example

Store a verbatim message.  When @var{content} is omitted, it is read
from stdin.  @code{--role} defaults to @code{note} (alternatives:
@code{user}, @code{assistant}, @code{system}).

@cindex dry run
With @code{--dry-run}, the message is validated and the would-be record
shown, without writing anything; the machine envelope carries
@code{"dry_run": true}.

@cindex supersession
@cindex bi-temporal validity
With @code{--supersedes @var{id}}, the new memory replaces an earlier
one in the @emph{same scope}: in one transaction the target's validity
window is closed (@code{valid_to} set to the new record's timestamp,
@code{superseded_by} set to its id) and the replacement is stored.
Nothing is ever deleted --- reads simply default to currently-valid
rows.  Outcomes: an unknown or cross-scope target exits 3; a rule
target exits 2 (rules have their own lifecycle); an already-superseded
target exits 5 with a @code{CONFLICT} error naming the winner.

@section engram recall

@cindex recall command

@example
engram recall --scope @var{id} [--limit @var{n}] [--budget-tokens @var{n}] [--as-of @var{instant} | --include-superseded]
@end example

Read back the last @var{n} messages for a scope, in chronological
order.  @code{--limit} defaults to 50.

@cindex time travel
@cindex as-of queries
By default only currently-valid memories are returned (superseded rows
are hidden).  @code{--as-of @var{instant}} (ISO 8601 UTC, e.g.@:
@code{2026-08-01T12:00:00Z}) time-travels to the memories that were
valid at that instant; @code{--include-superseded} returns the full
verbatim history.  The two are mutually exclusive.  @command{engram
search} accepts the same pair.

@cindex budget, token
@cindex token budget
With @code{--budget-tokens}, the results are packed to a token budget:
the newest messages have priority, so when the budget bites it is the
@emph{oldest} that are dropped, and the surviving messages are still
returned in chronological order.  Token costs are estimated as
@code{ceil(characters / 4)} (minimum 1) --- a multi-model pipeline has
no single correct tokenizer, so Engram uses this crude heuristic and
names it in the response: the envelope gains @code{metadata.budget}
with @code{estimator} (@code{"chars-div-4"}),
@code{requested_tokens}, @code{estimated_tokens} (included items
only), @code{included}, @code{dropped}, @code{dropped_ids}, and
per-channel candidate counts under @code{channels}.  Without the flag,
the output is byte-identical to earlier releases.

@section engram search

@cindex search command

@example
engram search @var{query} [--scope @var{id}] [--limit @var{n}] [--budget-tokens @var{n}] [--mode fts|hybrid]
@end example

Full-text (FTS5) search across stored memories, optionally restricted
to one scope.  @code{--limit} defaults to 20.  Query tokens are
sanitized --- each is matched as a quoted phrase, and tokens are joined
with @code{OR} so a natural-language query with one non-matching filler
word still finds its document (BM25 ranks multi-token matches first) ---
so arbitrary input cannot inject FTS5 syntax.

@cindex hybrid search
@cindex vector search
@cindex semantic search
In a build with the @code{vector} feature, @code{--mode hybrid} fuses
the FTS5 channel with a Model2Vec cosine-similarity channel --- and,
since M4, with the extracted-fact channel (@pxref{Invocation,,engram
consolidate}) --- via reciprocal rank fusion (k=60).  The facts channel
boosts memories whose marker-prefixed decision lines match the query;
because facts are verbatim substrings of content, it can never surface
a memory plain FTS5 could not.  With @code{--mode} omitted, hybrid
engages automatically when the feature is compiled in, a local model
resolves (@code{--model-path}, then @env{ENGRAM_MODEL}, then
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/engram/model}), and the model has indexed vectors
(run @command{engram index} first); otherwise the search is plain FTS5.  An
explicit @code{--mode hybrid} with a missing prerequisite is a
structured error (exit code 2), never a silent fallback.  Engram never
downloads a model.  The measured gate for shipping this feature is
recorded in @file{bench/RESULTS.md}.

@section engram index

@cindex index command
@cindex embeddings, backfilling

@example
engram index [--scope @var{id}] [--batch @var{n}] [--dry-run]
@end example

Backfills vector embeddings (under the resolved local model) for every
memory that has none yet --- including memories written by surfaces
that never embed live (MCP, HTTP) and history predating the model.
Rule rows are never embedded: policy is delivered through the rules
section of @command{engram context}, not through retrieval.  Requires
a build with the @code{vector} feature and a local Model2Vec model
directory.

@section engram context

@cindex context command
@cindex session start
@cindex reciprocal rank fusion

@example
engram context [--scope @var{id}] [--query @var{q}] [--budget-tokens @var{n}] [--limit @var{n}]
@end example

Assemble a budget-packed context block for session start: the scope's
active rules first, then memories fused from recency and relevance.
@code{--budget-tokens} defaults to 3000; @code{--limit} (candidates
fetched per channel) defaults to 50.  @code{--scope} resolves through
the same cascade as the @command{rule} commands (@pxref{Rules}).

The rules are @emph{always all included}, even when they alone exceed
the budget --- policy is never silently dropped; the overrun is
visible in the budget report.  Memories are then packed into whatever
budget remains.

Without @code{--query}, memories are selected newest-first.  With
@code{--query}, the recency channel (newest first), the full-text
relevance channel (rank order), and the extracted-fact channel (the
parents of matching facts, deduped --- see @command{engram
consolidate} below) are fused with reciprocal rank fusion
(@code{k = 60}), so an old-but-relevant memory can displace a
new-but-irrelevant one, and the memory that @emph{states} a decision
outranks the ones that merely mention its words.  The budget report
names each channel's candidate count (@code{channels.facts} included).
Selection order and presentation order
differ by design: whichever way a memory earned its slot, the
included memories are presented chronologically, which is how a
prompt reads best.  Rule rows surfaced by recall or search are
skipped as candidates --- they already lead the block.

The data carries @code{rules}, @code{memories}, @code{rules_tokens},
and the @code{budget} report; the envelope repeats the report as
@code{metadata.budget}.

@section engram consolidate

@cindex consolidate command
@cindex facts, extracted
@cindex fact extraction
@cindex deduplication
@cindex decay report
@cindex contradictions

@example
engram consolidate [--extract] [--dedup [--yes]] [--report]
                   [--scope @var{id}] [--dry-run]
@end example

Idle-time maintenance, in three combinable phases; at least one phase
flag is required (running @command{consolidate} bare is an error, exit
code 2).  The data payload carries one optional section per phase that
ran: @code{extract}, @code{dedup}, @code{report}.  All phases are
optionally narrowed to one scope; @code{--scope} omitted walks
@emph{every} scope, deliberately unlike the rule commands' cascade,
because maintenance naturally spans the whole database.

@subsection consolidate --extract

@code{--extract} runs the deterministic extractor
(@code{deterministic-v1}) over every @emph{current, non-rule} memory.
@code{--dry-run} reports what would be written without writing (it is
meaningful only for this phase; the others are already read-only
without @code{--yes}).

@cindex markers, fact
Extraction is pure string processing --- @emph{no LLM ever runs on the
write path}.  Content is split into lines (plus sentence-splits of
multi-sentence lines); after trimming bullet markers, a unit is a fact
iff it starts (case-insensitively) with one of the markers
@code{Decided:}, @code{Decision:}, @code{TODO}, @code{FIXME},
@code{NOTE:}, @code{Rule:}, @code{Fix:}, @code{Fixed:}, @code{Chose:},
@code{Chosen:}, @code{Rejected:}, @code{Constraint:}, @code{Gotcha:},
@code{Warning:}, @code{Never}, @code{Always}, @code{Must},
@code{Do not}, or @code{Don't}.  Facts shorter than 12 characters are
skipped, exact duplicates collapse, and at most @emph{eight} facts per
memory are kept (the first eight in document order).

Facts are stored @emph{verbatim} --- each is an exact substring of its
parent memory, with the parent's id as a drill-down pointer
(@code{GET /v1/memory/:id}, the MCP @code{get} tool) --- and they
never replace the verbatim record: they are an index over it.  Ids are
deterministic (UUID v5 over the memory id and the fact text), so
re-running extraction upserts in place --- the pass is idempotent.  A
fact's liveness derives from its @emph{parent}: superseding a memory
retires its facts at query time (the fact channel joins the parent and
applies the validity filter there); nothing is ever deleted.

@subsection consolidate --dedup

@cindex near-duplicates
@cindex winner, dedup

@code{--dedup} finds groups of near-duplicate @emph{current, non-rule}
memories within each scope.  Two detectors run and their pair edges
are @emph{unioned} into groups (connected components):

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{exact} --- normalized text equality: trimmed, lowercased,
internal whitespace collapsed to single spaces.  Always on.

@item
@emph{vector} --- cosine similarity of at least 0.92 between
@emph{stored} embeddings, same-scope pairs only.  Runs exactly when
the auto-hybrid gate would pass (vector feature built in, a local
model resolves, and that model has indexed vectors); otherwise the
exact detector runs alone.
@end itemize

Each group keeps its @emph{newest} row (latest @code{created_at}, id
as tie-break) as the winner.  Without @code{--yes} the phase is
report-only: the payload names each group's winner and the ids that
would be superseded.  With @code{--yes}, every loser's validity window
is closed in favor of the winner --- exactly the bi-temporal
supersession semantics of @code{remember --supersedes}
(@code{valid_to} plus @code{superseded_by}), except that no new row is
inserted, because the winner already exists.  Dedup @emph{never}
deletes: the losers stay reachable through
@code{--include-superseded} and @code{--as-of}.  The pass is
idempotent --- superseded losers are no longer current, so a second
run finds nothing.

@subsection consolidate --report

@cindex staleness
@cindex negation markers

@code{--report} is always report-only and produces two sections over
the current, non-rule memories.

@emph{Contradictions}: pairs of same-scope memories whose lowercased
word sets overlap with a Jaccard index of at least 0.5 @emph{and}
where exactly one of the pair contains a negation marker
(@code{not}, @code{never}, @code{no longer}, @code{don't},
@code{do not}, @code{isn't}, @code{wasn't}, @code{stopped} --- each
matched with its trailing space, so @samp{notable} never matches
@code{not}).  This is a deliberately
crude heuristic --- it will both miss real contradictions and flag
harmless rephrasings --- so the tool @emph{never} auto-resolves: a
human or agent inspects each pair and, where the contradiction is
real, records the truth with @code{engram remember --supersedes}.

@emph{Decay}: every memory is scored
@code{staleness = age_days * 1.0 + 30.0 / (1 + access_count)} ---
crude, but monotone in both age and un-accessedness --- and the top 20
are returned with their age, access count, and last access time.  The
access counters are maintained by the tracked read paths
(@pxref{Invocation, , the @code{--no-track} global option}).

@subsection Surface posture

The command is deliberately @emph{CLI-only} --- it exists on neither
the MCP nor the HTTP surface.  Extraction, dedup, and reporting are an
operator's batch jobs; agents receive facts automatically through the
@command{engram context} fusion and hybrid search ranking, and act on
dedup/contradiction findings only through the ordinary memory
commands.

@section engram rule

@cindex rule command

Durable project rules --- policy that outlives a session
(@pxref{Rules} for the concept and invariants).

@subsection rule add

@example
engram rule add --id @var{kebab-id} [--scope @var{id}] [--agent @var{name}] [@var{text}]
@end example

Record a rule, or revise the existing rule with the same @code{--id}
(an upsert --- never a second, competing copy).  @var{text} is read
from stdin when omitted.  @code{--agent} defaults to
@env{ENGRAM_AGENT}, else @code{engram-cli}.  Storing a rule does not
surface it to anyone --- follow with @command{engram rule sync}.

@subsection rule list

@example
engram rule list [--scope @var{id}] [--include-retired]
@end example

List the rules in effect for a scope, ordered by id.
@code{--include-retired} also shows withdrawn rules, flagged with
@code{"retired": true}.

@subsection rule retire

@example
engram rule retire --id @var{kebab-id} [--scope @var{id}]
@end example

Withdraw a rule so it stops being binding.  The rule is tombstoned, not
deleted (@pxref{Rules}).  Retiring an unknown id is an error (exit
code 3).

@subsection rule purge

@cindex purge, rule
@cindex deleting a rule

@example
engram rule purge --id @var{kebab-id} [--scope @var{id}] --yes [--dry-run]
@end example

Permanently delete a @emph{retired} rule's row --- the one true delete
in Engram, and deliberately CLI-only: it exists on neither the MCP nor
the HTTP surface, because destructive operations are not
agent-invocable.  An active rule is refused (exit 2; retire it first),
an unknown id exits 3, and without @code{--yes} nothing is deleted
(the process may not have a TTY to ask on, so consent must be
explicit).  @code{--dry-run} previews the deletion.

@subsection rule sync

@example
engram rule sync [--scope @var{id}] [--file @var{path}]@dots{} [--dry-run]
@end example

Render the scope's rules into @file{AGENTS.md}
(or the given @code{--file} targets, repeatable).  Only the region
between the engram sentinels is rewritten.  With @code{--dry-run},
reports what would be written without touching any file.

@section engram mcp

@cindex mcp command

@example
engram mcp [--db @var{path}]
@end example

Run as an MCP server over stdio.  @xref{MCP Server}.

@section engram serve

@cindex serve command

@example
engram serve [--addr @var{ip:port}] [--db @var{path}]
@end example

Run the HTTP API.  @code{--addr} defaults to @code{127.0.0.1:8420}.
@xref{HTTP API}.

