@section engram save-chat

@cindex save-chat command
@cindex chat history, exporting

@example
engram save-chat [--scope @var{id}] [--file @var{path}] [--model @var{name}]
                 [--dry-run]
@end example

Archive a scope's history as a complete Texinfo document.
@code{--file} defaults to @file{chat/@var{timestamp}.texi} and, when
relative, resolves against the project root rather than the process's
current directory --- so the command targets the same file from any
subdirectory.  @code{--model} records the archiving model or agent name
(falling back to @env{MODEL}, @env{LLM_MODEL}, @env{AI_AGENT}, then
@env{AGENT}).  @file{chat/} is added to the project's @file{.gitignore}
when absent, and the response says so explicitly rather than doing it
silently: it edits a tracked file.

@cindex gitignore, save-chat
The report is a @code{gitignore} object naming the file engram considered
(@code{path}), the entry it keeps (@code{entry}, @file{chat/}), what it did
(@code{action}), and a @code{detail} sentence naming engram as the actor.
The three actions are distinct, and only the first writes:

@table @code
@item added
Engram appended the entry.
@item already-ignored
The entry was already present; engram wrote nothing.
@item would-add
A dry run: engram would have appended the entry, and wrote nothing.
@end table

@noindent
This replaces an earlier boolean @code{gitignore_updated}, which left the
reader to supply who had acted and on which file, reported @code{false} for
the ordinary no-op in a way that read as a failure, and reported @code{true}
for a dry run that had written nothing at all.

@cindex idempotence, save-chat
The rendered document is a @emph{pure function} of the scope's contents,
exactly as the @command{engram rule sync} block is (@pxref{Rules}).
Re-running @command{save-chat} over an unchanged scope therefore produces
byte-identical output, the write is skipped, and the response reports
@code{outcome: "unchanged"}.  This is why the provenance header carries the
@emph{last message's} timestamp rather than the export's wall clock: a
wall-clock stamp would rewrite the file on every invocation.  An existing
archive is rewritten whole --- never appended to.

Two exclusions are deliberate.  Rules never appear in an archive: an
archive is a transcript, and rules have their own delivery mechanism.
Reads performed for an export do not bump access counters: archiving is
not retrieval, and counting it would corrupt the decay signal that
@command{engram consolidate --report} computes.  Superseded rows @emph{are}
included --- an archive records what was said, not what is currently true.

@section engram ingest

@cindex ingest command
@cindex transcript capture
@cindex harness transcripts

@example
engram ingest [--harness @var{name}] [--session @var{id}|latest|all]
              [--scope @var{id}] [--cwd @var{path}]
              [--include-thinking] [--include-tools] [--include-sidechains]
              [--max-bytes @var{n}] [--max-chars-per-turn @var{n}]
              [--list] [--dry-run]
@end example

Capture a harness's own session transcript into a scope, storing each
message as an ordinary memory with role @code{user} or @code{assistant}.
Those two role values have been in the schema since the beginning; until
this command existed, nothing ever wrote them, because the only way into
the store was an agent deciding to call @command{remember}.

The harness is resolved from @code{--harness}, then from the environment
marker of the harness engram is running under, then --- only when exactly
one installed harness has a reader --- from that one.  Two candidates is
an error.  Engram does not guess which conversation to copy into a scope.

@subsection What is dropped

@cindex filtering, transcript
A session is mostly not prose.  Measured on one real 1.7@tie{}MB session:
935 records in, @strong{46 turns out} --- 140 tool calls, 139 tool
results, 52 thinking blocks, 226 non-message records.  Tool payloads and
model thinking are therefore excluded by default, and everything dropped
is counted and reported under @code{filtered}.

Tool payloads are @emph{never} stored, even with @code{--include-tools}:
a tool result is summarized to its size.  Payloads are where file
contents, command output, and credentials live, and copying them into a
database shared by every model on the machine is the largest privacy
exposure engram could create.  What survives is additionally passed
through a credential scrubber, whose replacements are counted per kind in
the response.  That scrubber is a best-effort net, not a guarantee.

@subsection Never guess

Harness transcript formats are private, undocumented, and change without
notice.  Two consequences are deliberate:

@itemize @bullet
@item
An unrecognized record type increments @code{filtered.unknown_record}
rather than being skipped quietly.  A format change then shows up as a
count instead of as a silently shorter capture.
@item
An unparseable timestamp is an error, never a substitution of the current
time.  @command{recall} orders by @code{created_at}, so a wall-clock
fallback would collapse an entire conversation into one instant and
destroy its reading order invisibly.
@end itemize

For the same reason, a harness engram cannot read fails with exit
status@tie{}2 and a hint naming the fallback --- call @command{remember}
during the session, then @command{save-chat} --- and writes nothing at
all to standard output.  An empty success would be indistinguishable from
``there is nothing here.''

@subsection Idempotence

Each turn's id is a version@tie{}5 UUID over the harness, session, and
source record, the same discipline as the fact index uses.  Re-ingesting
a session therefore inserts nothing, and resuming a live one inserts only
the new tail.  Idempotence falls out of the identifier rather than out of
bookkeeping about which files have been seen.

That places real weight on what counts as a ``session.''  Codex reuses one
identifier across every rollout of a resumed conversation --- three files
sharing one were found on a single machine --- so engram keys on the
rollout's file name instead.  Had it trusted the recorded identifier, two
turns at the same position in different rollouts would have derived the
same id, and the second would have been discarded as already present.
Losing messages quietly is the one behaviour this command must not have.

@subsection Supported harnesses

@cindex Claude Code transcripts
@cindex Codex transcripts
Readers exist for Claude Code and Codex.  They locate a session quite
differently, and neither approach generalizes to the other:

@table @asis
@item Claude Code
Transcripts live under a directory named for the working directory, with
each separator rewritten as a dash.  That mapping is applied forwards
only: a directory name may itself contain a dash, so the inverse does not
exist and is not offered.

@item Codex
The directory tree records the date rather than the working directory, so
there is nothing to reverse.  Each rollout opens with a record stating its
working directory verbatim, and listing reads exactly that one line from
each file.
@end table

A rollout carries the conversation twice: once as the events the interface
displayed, and once as the raw exchange with the model.  Engram prefers the
displayed events, and not merely because they are simpler to read.  The raw
channel is @emph{noisier}: on a real rollout it held one more user message
than the display channel, and that message was an environment block the
harness had injected rather than anything a person wrote.  The raw channel
remains a fallback, used when a rollout carries no displayed conversation
at all, so a change that retires one representation degrades rather than
silently producing nothing.

@cindex transcript size
Transcripts can be very large --- one rollout on a developer machine was
114@tie{}MB.  Both readers stream line by line, and
@code{--max-bytes} (64@tie{}MiB by default) refuses an oversized
transcript with an error naming the override rather than attempting it.

@section engram install

@cindex install command
@cindex slash commands
@cindex harness commands

@example
engram install [--harness @var{name}]@dots{} [--db-path @var{path}]
               [--list] [--dry-run] [--force]
@end example

Write engram's slash commands --- @code{/engram-save-chat},
@code{/engram-ingest}, and @code{/engram-context} --- into the command
directory each harness loads for itself.  Engram is typically already
registered as an MCP server everywhere; what this adds is a command
surface, so engram can be invoked by name from inside a session.

Begin with @code{--list}, which reports every harness engram knows,
whether it is installed, where its commands would go, and which database
it already registered engram against, and writes nothing.

@subsection What it will and will not touch

@itemize @bullet
@item
Only @emph{detected} harnesses.  Engram does not create a home directory
for software that is not installed.
@item
Only files carrying engram's generated-by banner.  A command file written
by hand is reported as skipped, with the reason, and left exactly as it
was unless @code{--force} is given.
@item
Nothing is ever deleted, and no file outside a harness's command
directory is touched.
@end itemize

The operation is idempotent by byte comparison: running it twice reports
every file @code{unchanged} and does not so much as alter a modification
time.  The banner deliberately carries no version number, because
embedding one would make every release rewrite every installed file.

@subsection The database is discovered, not assumed

@cindex database discovery
Each generated command pins @code{--db} explicitly, to the path that
harness already registered engram against.  This matters more than it
looks: the harnesses on a machine normally share one database, and a
command that omitted the flag would fall back to a relative default and
quietly write somewhere else.

Configuration files are scanned narrowly rather than deserialized ---
JSON, JSON-with-comments, and TOML each by their own small reader, so
engram needs no TOML dependency and does not choke on comments.  Engram
only ever @emph{reads} these files.  It will not rewrite a commented
configuration, because a round-trip through a serializer would silently
delete the comments.

@subsection Harnesses without a command surface

Of the harnesses engram knows, only some load user-written commands from
a directory it can write.  The rest are reported plainly as having no
command surface rather than omitted from the output.  Claiming otherwise
would make the feature look broken on the harnesses it was never able to
reach.

@cindex Nix, home-manager
On a declaratively managed home, skill directories are read-only symlinks
into the Nix store, which is why engram ships commands rather than a
skill.  When a target resolves into the store, @command{install} warns
that the next @command{home-manager switch} will replace whatever it
writes; reference the plugin directory from the configuration instead.

@subsection Why this is not an HTTP route

@command{install} writes into the user's home directory, a step beyond
anything else engram does --- @command{rule sync} stays inside the
project root.  The HTTP surface has no authentication, which already
means any local process can rewrite a project's @file{AGENTS.md}.
Exposing @command{install} there would extend that reach to the home
directory, and eventually to code executed at the end of every session.
There is no route, and there should not be one.

@node MCP Server
@chapter MCP Server

@cindex MCP server
@cindex Model Context Protocol
@cindex stdio transport

@command{engram mcp} runs a Model Context Protocol server over stdio,
suitable for wiring into Claude Code, Codex, and other MCP clients:

@example
engram mcp --db /path/to/engram.db
@end example

The server exposes ten tools.  The memory tools mirror the CLI commands
of the same names; the rule tools mirror @command{engram rule}.

@cindex tool ceiling (MCP)
The tool surface is deliberately narrow: every tool's schema costs
context on every turn of every conversation, so Engram budgets a hard
ceiling of ten --- and @strong{that ceiling is now reached}.  An
eleventh tool must displace an existing one, and the displacement must
be argued here rather than decided in passing.

@code{save_chat} earned the last slot only because it carries both
halves of the capture story: it can capture the session transcript and
then archive it.  Spending the slot on archiving alone would have left
the server able to export a conversation but never to record one, with
no slot left to fix that.

Several commands are deliberately absent and are not candidates for the
last slot.  @command{engram install} writes into the user's home
directory.  @command{engram consolidate} and @command{engram index} are
operator batch jobs whose results agents receive automatically through
ranking.  @command{engram rule purge} deletes, and destructive
operations are not agent-invocable.

@table @code
@item remember
@cindex remember tool (MCP)
Store a verbatim chat message in shared memory, scoped to a
project/task/run id.  @code{role} defaults to @code{note}.

@item recall
@cindex recall tool (MCP)
Read back the last N memories for a scope, in chronological order
(default limit 50).

@item search
@cindex search tool (MCP)
Full-text search across stored memories, optionally restricted to one
scope (default limit 20).

@item get
@cindex get tool (MCP)
Fetch a single memory by id --- the drill-down half of search/recall.
Looks across the full history, superseded rows included: an id lookup
answers ``what is this row'', not ``what is true now''.

@item context
@cindex context tool (MCP)
Assemble a budget-packed session-start block: the scope's active rules
first (always included), then recency+relevance fused memories packed
to the token budget (default 3000).  One call replaces separate
@code{recall} + @code{rule_list} at session start.

@item rule_add
@cindex rule_add tool (MCP)
Record a durable project rule, or revise the existing rule with the
same @code{rule_id}.  @code{agent} defaults to @code{mcp-client}.

@item rule_list
@cindex rule_list tool (MCP)
List the durable rules in effect for a scope, ordered by
@code{rule_id}.  Call at session start to load standing policy.

@item rule_retire
@cindex rule_retire tool (MCP)
Withdraw a rule so it stops being binding.  Tombstones rather than
deletes.

@item rule_sync
@cindex rule_sync tool (MCP)
Render a scope's rules into @file{AGENTS.md} at
the project root.

@item save_chat
@cindex save_chat tool (MCP)
Archive a scope's history as a Texinfo document under the project's
@file{chat/} directory, optionally capturing this harness's own session
transcript into the scope first (@code{from_transcript}).

@cindex path traversal, MCP
There is deliberately no @code{file} argument.  The destination is
derived from the server's resolved project root and cannot be
influenced by the caller --- the same omission, for the same reason, as
@code{rule_sync}'s missing @code{--file}.  A path argument here would be
a traversal primitive handed to a model whose input includes text an
attacker may have influenced: file contents, tool output, fetched pages.
The nine tools that preceded it write to exactly two places, the
database and sentinel-managed markdown at the project root; accepting an
arbitrary path would be the largest expansion of Engram's blast radius
to date, in exchange for a convenience nobody asked for.
@end table

@cindex scope, under MCP
One caution: the rule tools' scope cascade (@pxref{Rules}) resolves
against the @emph{server process's} working directory, not the
client's.  A shared server therefore needs an explicit @code{scope}
argument on every rule call.

@node HTTP API
@chapter HTTP API

@cindex HTTP API
@cindex routes

@command{engram serve} binds a local-only HTTP API --- default
@code{127.0.0.1:8420}, no authentication.  Do not expose it beyond the
loopback interface.

@cindex status codes
As of 0.2.0, @emph{all} routes return real HTTP status codes (a
deliberate breaking change: before 0.2.0 the @code{/v1/memory*}
handlers answered @code{200 OK} with an error body).  Success
responses carry the same metadata envelope as the CLI's machine mode.

@table @code
@item POST /v1/memory
@cindex POST /v1/memory
Store a memory.  Body: @code{@{"agent": @dots{}, "scope": @dots{},
"content": @dots{}@}} (optional @code{role}, optional
@code{supersedes}).  Returns @code{400} if @code{content} is empty or
whitespace-only, @code{200} with the stored Memory in the envelope on
success, @code{500} on a storage error.  With @code{supersedes}: the
target's validity window is closed in the same transaction; @code{404}
for an unknown or cross-scope target, @code{400} for a rule target,
and @code{409 Conflict} when the target was already superseded (the
message names the winning id).

@item GET /v1/memory/:id
@cindex GET /v1/memory/:id
Drill-down by id, across the full history (superseded rows included).
@code{404} when no such id exists.

@item GET /v1/memory/recall?scope=&limit=&budget_tokens=&as_of=&include_superseded=
@cindex GET /v1/memory/recall
Fetch the last N memories for a scope, chronological order.  Reads
default to currently-valid rows; @code{as_of} (ISO 8601 UTC)
time-travels and @code{include_superseded=true} shows the full history
(@code{400} if the pair is combined or the instant is malformed ---
@code{search} accepts the same parameters).  With
@code{budget_tokens}, results are packed to the token budget
(newest-first priority, chronological output) and the envelope carries
@code{metadata.budget} --- the same behavior as
@code{engram recall --budget-tokens} (@pxref{Invocation}).

@item GET /v1/memory/search?query=&scope=&limit=
@cindex GET /v1/memory/search
Full-text search, optionally scoped.  @code{budget_tokens} packs the
ranked results to a token budget, in rank order.

@item GET /v1/context?scope=&query=&budget_tokens=&limit=
@cindex GET /v1/context
A budget-packed context block for session start: active rules first
(always all included, even over budget), then recency+relevance fused
memories --- the same data shape as @command{engram context}
(@pxref{Invocation}).  @code{scope} resolves through the rule-command
cascade against the server process's working directory when omitted;
@code{budget_tokens} defaults to 3000 and @code{limit} to 50.  An
unknown scope yields @code{200} with empty rules and memories, not an
error.  Returns @code{500} on a storage failure.

@item POST /v1/rules
@cindex POST /v1/rules
Create or revise a rule (re-using a @code{rule_id} updates in place).
Returns @code{400} for an invalid id or text.

@item GET /v1/rules
@cindex GET /v1/rules
Active rules for a scope (@code{?scope=}); @code{?include_retired=}
adds tombstoned rules.

@item DELETE /v1/rules/:rule_id
@cindex DELETE /v1/rules/:rule_id
Retire (tombstone --- soft delete) a rule.  Returns @code{404} for an
unknown rule, @code{200} with the outcome on success.

@item POST /v1/rules/sync
@cindex POST /v1/rules/sync
Render the scope's rules into @file{AGENTS.md}.
This is the only route that writes outside the database; targets
derive from the server process's working directory, never from caller
input.

@item GET /v1/health
@cindex GET /v1/health
Liveness probe.
@end table

@node Environment Variables
@chapter Environment Variables

@cindex environment variables

@table @env
@item ENGRAM_DB
@cindex ENGRAM_DB
Database file path; overrides the @file{engram.db} default (the
@code{--db} flag overrides both).

@item ENGRAM_SCOPE
@cindex ENGRAM_SCOPE
Default scope for the @command{rule} commands, consulted by the scope
cascade (@pxref{Rules}).

@item ENGRAM_AGENT
@cindex ENGRAM_AGENT
Default @code{--agent} for @command{rule add} (falling back to
@code{engram-cli}).

@item AI_AGENT
@itemx AGENT
@cindex AI_AGENT
@cindex AGENT
Presence-based agent detection: set to any non-empty value, they
select machine output mode (and record @code{tool_agent} in the
envelope).

@item CI
@cindex CI
Truthy-based: selects machine output mode when set to a non-empty
value other than @code{0} or @code{false} (case-insensitive).

@item NO_COLOR
@cindex NO_COLOR
Set non-empty, disables colored output (same as @code{--no-color}).

@item SPACECRAFT_A11Y
@cindex SPACECRAFT_A11Y
Accessible output toggle (Standard @U{00A7}18): @code{0} is an explicit
off; any other non-empty value enables accessible mode.  The
@code{--accessible} flag always wins.
@end table

@node Rules
@chapter Rules

@cindex rules, durable
@cindex policy, durable

A memory records what happened; a @dfn{rule} states what must keep
being true.  Rules are durable project policy: stored once, revised in
place, and rendered into the markdown files agent harnesses load
automatically, so the policy keeps applying without anyone remembering
to restate it.  Rules are available on all three surfaces.

@section Invariants

@cindex invariants, rules
Four invariants worth not breaking:

@enumerate
@item
@cindex sync, as delivery
@emph{Sync is the delivery mechanism, not an export.}  A row in SQLite
never reaches a model's context.  Rendering into @file{AGENTS.md} ---
the file harnesses auto-load, and that Claude Code reaches through the
@code{@@AGENTS.md} import in its @file{CLAUDE.md} (Steelbore Standard
§5.7) --- is what makes a rule take effect.

@item
@emph{The rendered block is a pure function of the rules.}  No
generation timestamp; rules ordered by @code{rule_id}.  That makes
sync idempotent (unchanged rules mean no write) and therefore safe in
a hook or commit gate.

@item
@emph{Add upserts.}  Re-using a @code{rule_id} revises in place;
@code{created_at} survives, @code{updated_at} moves.  Two competing
copies of a rule are worse than none.

@item
@cindex tombstone
@emph{Retire tombstones, never deletes.}  A retired rule disappears
from listings and synced files but stays in the database and remains
searchable --- ``we used to require this'' is worth keeping.
Re-running @command{rule add} with the same id reinstates it.
Retiring is idempotent; an unknown id is an error (exit code 3 on the
CLI, @code{404} over HTTP), not a silent success.
@end enumerate

@section Sentinels

@cindex sentinels
@cindex managed block
The rendered block sits between HTML-comment sentinels; only that
region is ever rewritten, and everything outside it is preserved
verbatim:

@verbatim
<!-- engram:rules:begin ... -->
   ... rendered rules ...
<!-- engram:rules:end -->
@end verbatim

Rule text containing @code{engram:rules:} is rejected at write time
--- it would terminate its own block.  An opening sentinel with no
closing one is treated as a mangled block and replaced wholesale.

@section Scope cascade

@cindex scope cascade
@cindex scope_origin
When @code{--scope} is not given, the scope resolves through a
cascade, and the chosen origin is reported as @code{scope_origin}:

@enumerate
@item
The explicit @code{--scope} argument.
@item
The @env{ENGRAM_SCOPE} environment variable.
@item
The git working-tree basename.
@item
The current directory basename.
@end enumerate

Under MCP this cascade resolves against the server process's working
directory, so a shared server needs an explicit scope
(@pxref{MCP Server}).

@node Harness Integration
@chapter Harness Integration

@cindex harness integration
@cindex agent harnesses
Engram reaches outside itself in two directions.  @command{engram
ingest} @emph{reads} the session transcripts a harness writes for its own
purposes, and @command{engram install} @emph{writes} command files, and
optionally a hook, into the places a harness loads them from.  Both are
described under @ref{Invocation}; this chapter collects what governs them,
because the constraints are the interesting part and they are easy to
lose track of when they are scattered across command descriptions.

@section What Engram knows about each harness

@cindex harness registry
One table describes every harness, and detection is nothing more than
asking whether a path exists.  Engram never runs a subprocess to find a
harness, never reads a configuration file to decide whether one is
installed, and never creates a directory for one that is not.

Support is uneven, and the manual would rather say so than imply
otherwise:

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .26 .22 .30
@headitem Harness @tab Transcripts @tab Commands @tab Hooks
@item claude-code @tab read @tab written @tab written (opt-in)
@item codex @tab read @tab written @tab ---
@item opencode @tab --- @tab written @tab ---
@item qwen @tab --- @tab --- @tab ---
@item goose @tab --- @tab --- @tab ---
@item antigravity @tab --- @tab --- @tab ---
@item copilot-cli @tab --- @tab --- @tab ---
@end multitable

Every gap in that table is reported at runtime with its reason attached,
rather than omitted.  @command{engram install --list} names the harnesses
with no command surface; @command{engram ingest} fails with exit
status@tie{}2 and an explanation when asked to read a harness it cannot.
A feature that quietly does nothing on four of seven harnesses would read
as broken; one that says which four, and why, is merely honest about its
reach.

@section Formats Engram does not control

@cindex transcript format drift
@cindex format changes
Session transcripts are private, undocumented implementation details of
the tools that write them.  They will change without notice, and a reader
built on them has to expect that rather than assume it away.  Three
consequences run through the implementation:

@table @asis
@item Unrecognized records are counted, never skipped
Every record whose type Engram does not recognize increments
@code{filtered.unknown_record} in the response.  A format change then
announces itself as a number that was zero yesterday, instead of
disappearing as a capture that is quietly shorter than it should be.

This has already paid for itself twice.  It caught three kinds of tool
record that an early version of the Codex reader had miscategorized, and
it correctly flags genuinely truncated records --- one exists in the
middle of a real rollout, left by an interrupted write.

@item Unparseable timestamps are errors
Engram will not substitute the current time for one it cannot read.
@command{recall} orders by that field, so a wall-clock fallback would
collapse a conversation into a single instant and destroy its reading
order without any visible sign.

@item Nothing is inferred from a file name
Claude Code encodes a working directory in a directory name by replacing
each separator with a dash.  Engram applies that mapping forwards only:
a directory name may contain a dash of its own, so the inverse is not
well defined and is not offered.  Codex records its working directory
verbatim, so nothing needs reversing there.
@end table

@section Privacy

@cindex privacy, transcript capture
@cindex credentials, redaction
Capturing a transcript is a different act from recording a note, and the
difference deserves stating plainly.  When a model calls
@command{remember}, it has chosen a sentence.  When Engram ingests a
transcript, it copies whatever the session contained --- including
everything the user pasted into it.

That matters more than it might because the database is shared.  A
typical machine has every harness pointed at one file, several of them
backed by models running on somebody else's computer.  A credential that
lands in that file has effectively been handed to all of them.

Four measures follow, and none of them is decoration:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Tool payloads never enter.}  Not by default, and not with
@code{--include-tools} either --- that flag adds a one-line summary of
each tool call, and the payload is reduced to its size.  Payloads are
where file contents, command output, and environment files live, and on
a real session they are the majority of the transcript.

@item
@strong{Model reasoning is excluded by default.}

@item
@strong{What survives is scrubbed.}  Credential-shaped text is replaced
before storage, and the replacements are counted by kind in the response
so the user can see that it happened.  This is a best-effort net rather
than a guarantee: it recognizes the shapes of machine-issued tokens,
which are the ones that end up in transcripts by accident.  It cannot
recognize a password someone typed as prose.

@item
@strong{Capture is never automatic.}  There is no background ingestion.
The hook that can capture at the end of a session is installed only when
explicitly requested, and it captures rather than archives.
@end enumerate

@section Writing into a home directory

@cindex installation safety
@command{engram install} writes outside the project, which nothing else
in Engram does --- @command{engram rule sync} stays within the project
root.  The additional care that warrants:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Only harnesses that are actually installed are written to.
@item
Only files carrying Engram's own generated-by banner are overwritten.  A
command file written by hand is reported as skipped, with the reason.
@item
Nothing is deleted, and the operation is idempotent by byte comparison:
running it twice reports every file unchanged and does not alter a
modification time.
@item
The one structured file Engram merges into --- a settings file, and only
under @code{--hooks} --- is backed up first, has only its own hook entry
replaced, and is refused outright rather than overwritten if it does not
parse.  Other hooks on the same event are left where they are.
@end itemize

@cindex Nix, home-manager
On a declaratively managed home directory, skill directories are
read-only links into the Nix store.  That is why Engram ships commands
rather than a skill: command directories are writable.  When a target
resolves into the store, @command{install} says so, because the next
rebuild would replace whatever it wrote.

